{"cuisine/ainu-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ainu-cuisine","n":"Ainu cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Japan","intro":[["Ainu cuisine is the cuisine of the ethnic Ainu in Japan and Russia.  The cuisine differs markedly from that of the majority Yamato people of Japan.  Raw meat like sashimi, for example, is rarely served in Ainu cuisine, which instead uses methods such as boiling, roasting and curing to prepare meat. Also unlike Japanese cuisine, traditional Ainu cuisine did not use miso, soy sauce, or sugar, though these seasonings make an appearance in modern Ainu cuisine.  The island of Hokkaidō in northern Japan is where most Ainu live today; however, they once inhabited most of the Kuril Islands, the southern half of Sakhalin island, and parts of northern Honshū Island."],["There are very few Ainu restaurants in the world, though some do exist. Examples of Ainu restaurants include Haru Koro (Harukor) in Shinjuku, Tokyo, Ashiri Kotan Nakanoshima in Sapporo, and Poron'no and Marukibune in Akan, Hokkaidō."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ainu-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Munini-imo","dish/munini-imo"],["Ohaw","dish/ohaw"],["Rui-be","dish/rui-be"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Potato flour","ingredient/potato-flour"],["Potato starch","ingredient/potato-starch"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Kelp","ingredient/kelp"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1d/Ainu_Set_Meal.jpg/500px-Ainu_Set_Meal.jpg","w":500,"h":374,"by":"Jpatokal","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ainu_Set_Meal.jpg"}},"cuisine/teochew-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/teochew-cuisine","n":"Chaoshan (Teochew) cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Min cuisine","cuisine/fujian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Teochew cuisine, also known as Chiuchow cuisine, Chaozhou cuisine or Teo-swa cuisine, originated from the Chaoshan region in the eastern part of China's Guangdong Province, which includes the cities of Chaozhou, Shantou and Jieyang. Teochew cuisine bears more similarities to that of Fujian cuisine, particularly Southern Min cuisine, due to the similarity of Teochew's and Fujian's culture, language, and their geographic proximity to each other. However, Teochew cuisine is also influenced by Cantonese cuisine in its style and technique."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"teochew-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teochew_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Min cuisine","cuisine/fujian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Teochew Heritage Cooking","ref":"cookbook/teochew-heritage-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Eric Low, 2015","note":"History and 50 recipes of Singapore's Teochew community, by a Singapore chef.","url":"https://www.marshallcavendish.com/our-books/categories/books/teochew-heritage-cooking-p9789814634281","suggested":true,"isbn":"9789814634281"},{"name":"Uncle Lau's Teochew Recipes","ref":"cookbook/uncle-lau-s-teochew-recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Tan Lee Leng","note":"Singapore heritage cookbook of Teochew home recipes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9789810733377","suggested":true,"isbn":"9789810733377"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Crab jujube","dish/crab-jujube"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Min cuisine","cuisine/fujian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4c/Fried_crab_dumpling_%28hoicho%29_-_Bangkok_-_2017-10-01.jpg/500px-Fried_crab_dumpling_%28hoicho%29_-_Bangkok_-_2017-10-01.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Iudexvivorum","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fried_crab_dumpling_(hoicho)_-_Bangkok_-_2017-10-01.jpg","of":"Crab jujube"}},"cuisine/viennese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/viennese-cuisine","n":"Viennese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Austria","intro":[["Viennese cuisine is the cuisine of Vienna, Austria. While elements of it have spread throughout the country, other regions have their own variations of Austrian cuisine."],["Viennese cuisine is known for Wiener schnitzel and pastries, but includes a wide range of other dishes."],["Wiener schnitzel (veal coated in breadcrumbs and fried), Tafelspitz (boiled beef), Beuschel (a ragout containing veal lungs and heart), and Selchfleisch (smoked meat) with sauerkraut and dumplings are typical of its cooking. Sweet Viennese dishes include Apfelstrudel (strudel pastry filled with apples), Millirahmstrudel (milk-cream strudel), Kaiserschmarrn (shredded pancakes served with fruit compotes), and Sachertorte (cake of two layers of chocolate cake with apricot jam in the middle). These and other desserts on offer at the Konditorei of Vienna are generally eaten with coffee in the afternoon. Liptauer, a spread, and Powidl, a base for dumplings, are also popular."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"viennese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viennese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Plachutta: original recipes","ref":"site/plachutta-at","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"plachutta.at","note":"Recipes from the Plachutta family's Viennese restaurants, known for Tafelspitz.","url":"https://www.plachutta.at/en/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Plachutta: Viennese Cuisine","ref":"cookbook/plachutta-viennese-cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Ewald Plachutta, Mario Plachutta","note":"English edition of the Plachutta family's collection of classic Viennese dishes.","url":"https://www.plachutta.at/en/shop-books/produkt/3/plachutta-viennese-cuisine-authentic-austrian-recipes.html","suggested":true},{"name":"The Viennese Kitchen: Tante Hertha's Book of Family Recipes","ref":"cookbook/the-viennese-kitchen-tante-hertha-s-book-of-family-recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Monica Meehan, Maria von Baich","note":"Family recipes of a Viennese household, from schnitzel to tortes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781504800709","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781504800709"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Goulash","dish/goulash"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Agaricus","ingredient/agaricus"],["Allspice","ingredient/allspice"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/40/Strudel.jpg/500px-Strudel.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"che","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strudel.jpg"}},"ingredient/marshmallow":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/marshmallow","n":"Marshmallow","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Marshmallow is a confection made from sugar, water and gelatin whipped to a solid-but-soft consistency. It is used as a filling in baking or molded into shapes and coated with corn starch. This sugar confection is inspired by a medicinal confection made from Althaea officinalis, the marsh-mallow plant."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-marshmallow"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshmallow"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states",[["Ambrosia (fruit salad)","dish/ambrosia-fruit-salad"],["Moon Pie","dish/moon-pie"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Mandarin orange","ingredient/mandarin-orange"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"],["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Graham cracker","ingredient/graham-cracker"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3d/White_Marshmallows.jpg/500px-White_Marshmallows.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"John Morgan","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Marshmallows.jpg"}},"dish/aspic":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/aspic","n":"Aspic","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 8 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Aspic () or meat jelly is a savory gelatin made with a meat stock or broth, set in a mold to encase other ingredients. These often include pieces of meat, seafood, vegetable, or eggs. Aspic is also sometimes referred to as aspic gelée or aspic jelly. In its simplest form, aspic is essentially a gelatinous version of conventional soup."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-aspic"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspic"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine"],["Estonian cuisine","cuisine/estonian-cuisine"],["French cuisin